          equipment: 'Poker chips, Dealer button, Small/Big Blind markers.',
          dealing: '5 cards face down to each player. 3 draw phases follow.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win half the pot with the best 4-card Badugi and half with the best 5-card Deuce-to-Seven low.',
          gameplay: '1. **Blinds**: Initial stakes. 2. **Deal**: 5 cards. 3. **Betting**. 4. **Three Draws**: Discard/draw rounds punctuated by betting. 5. **Showdown**: Split resolution.',
          keyRules: [
            'Aces count as high (above Kings) and are essentially useless in a winning hand.',
            'Straights and Flushes are active: if you hold 2-3-4-5-6 of mixed suits, you have a straight (bad for low), but you have a 4-card Badugi (2-3-4-5).',
            'If you hold five hearts, your Lowball half is a Flush and will lose to almost any non-flush hand.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Avoid the Ace. In Badeucy, the Ace is a 'dead card' for your low hand. If you start with an Ace, discard it immediately unless it's part of a desperate Badugi bluff.",
          intermediate: "Manage suits carefully. Unlike Badacey where flushes don't matter, in Badeucy you must ACTIVELY AVOID flushes for your Lowball half. This means your 4-card Badugi (mixed suits) is naturally aligned with a good Lowball hand.",
          advanced: "Range Polarization: Because straights and flushes are 'bad', you can tell a lot about an opponent's hand by how many cards they draw. Specifically, a player drawing zero cards ('Standing Pat') is very likely to have a 7-low or 8-low that is NOT a straight.",
          tips: [
            "The 'Hammer': A hand like 2-3-4-5-7 rainbow is the ultimate weapon in this game.",
            "If you have four cards to a wheel (2-3-4-5), but two are the same suit, your Badugi is technically only 3 cards (2-3-4). This is a 'Rough' hand; don't overplay it.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
